nodecode-spotify is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins
  commit e7a218f26db88426ab3e7376f0e000b6e356f5e7 (the spotify/ folder)
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

The rewrite keeps what the plugin gave a user: the seven tools over Spotify's
Web API, with the same names, actions and arguments -- playback, devices, the
queue, search, playlists, albums and the library -- along with the id/URI/link
normalization, the player command set, the "play a bare list of tracks inside
its album" request Spotify's desktop client needs, the 204 that means nothing
is playing, the friendly 401/403/404/429 sentences, and one forced token
refresh (before a call that is about to expire, and once on a 401). Everything
is Common Lisp over the cell contract: NLK:HTTP for the wire, NLK:DECODE-JSON
for the bodies, and one NLE:DEFINE-CELL declaration for the section, the seven
tools, the /spotify command and the manual.

Left out, and why: the loopback callback listener, with its self-signed TLS
pair and its private hermes_cli imports. A cell that opened a port would need
threads, a certificate and a browser launch inside the harness, and the state
it protects -- one code -- is in the address the browser already shows. So
/spotify login prints the authorize URL and /spotify login <that address>
finishes the exchange; no port is opened, and the flow works over SSH. The
`hermes spotify login|status|logout` CLI is gone, its three verbs now the
/spotify command's. Tokens live in a 0600 JSON file under the home
(spotify/auth.json) rather than in Hermes's auth.json, since that store is
Hermes's own.
